      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2012 European Federation of International Association for the Study of Pain Chapters.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "Hypertension has been associated with hypoalgesia. This prospective study was designed to test the effects of untreated preoperative essential hypertension on post-operative pain intensity and morphine requirement after major abdominal surgery."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "Sixty subjects (30 untreated essential hypertensives and 30 normotensives) scheduled for abdominal surgery were included in this study. All subjects received standardized anaesthetic with intra-operative fentanyl and patient-controlled analgesia with morphine for 48\u2009h post-operatively as the only analgesics. Pain intensity scores, cumulative morphine requirement and side effects were recorded until 48\u2009h post-operatively."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "All subjects with essential hypertension had systolic hypertension, 93.3% had grade 1 severity and 6.7% had grade 2 severity. 23.3% of essential hypertensive subjects had elevated diastolic blood pressure. Essential hypertensive subjects had significantly lower total post-operative morphine requirement (29.6\u2009mg vs. 49.9\u2009mg; p\u2009=\u20090.002), significantly lower verbal rating scale post-operative pain intensity scores at rest and with coughing (p\u2009=\u20090.000), and significantly less incidence of post-operative pruritus (p\u2009=\u20090.048) over 48\u2009h than normotensive subjects. There were no post-operative differences in the incidence of post-operative nausea (p\u2009=\u20090.982) or vomiting (p\u2009=\u20090.644) between the two groups."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "Untreated essential hypertension is associated with significantly reduced post-operative morphine requirement and pain intensity, suggesting hypertension-associated hypoalgesia."
          }
        ]
      },
